Hi [~accountid:638713ee77acd224b3411ff9] in summary please see below the recommendations from outside attorney:

*Bolt would like to know what would be a safe value to propose that would not be considered a “**_valor anormalmente baixo_**” and that would still be low enough for Bolt to be awarded the points for the lowest value in contest*.

* There is no supplementary legal criterion for classifying a given price as abnormally low;
* No value has been specified in the tender documents as being likely to constitute an abnormally low price;
* The classification of a price as abnormally low, in this case, depends on a value judgement to be made by the contracting authority, on a case-by-case basis, and it is not possible to put forward any value that offers guarantees that a certain price it will not be considered abnormally low;
* In the past, there was a supplementary criterion which determined that, in the context of contracts for the purchase or lease of goods or the purchase of services, a price 50% or more lower than the base price should be considered abnormally low;
* In the event that the price submitted by BOLT is considered abnormally low, there is no automatic exclusion of the tender, but BOLT must be called upon to present evidence justifying the submission of that price, which will be followed by a decision by the contracting authority as to whether that price can be accepted or whether it should determine the exclusion of the tender;
* The decision on whether or not to exclude a tender with an abnormally low price will thus depend on the acceptance of the explanations submitted by the tenderers;
* The outside attorney would +*not recommend submitting a price of €0.00*+, as the Code contains certain provisions aimed at excluding supplies free of charge which, even if they did not lead to the exclusion of the bid submitted, could constitute limitations for future procedures (+*preventing BOLT from being invited to submit a bid in certain direct award procedures or prior consultations organised by the local authority*+).    

*Bolt will not be ready to initiate activity within the 30-day post-award period defined by the contract. We know this implies a fine for each day of delay and that is ok from a financial standpoint. Although, we would like you to confirm all possible consequences of not sticking with the deadline for service initiation defined in the contract – meaning, will it have an impact on licences, on the possibility to apply to other public tenders or anything else?*

* Failure to comply with deadlines may result in the application of +contractual penalties+;
* Failure to meet deadlines may also result in the +cancellation of the contract+;
* The application of contractual penalties in an amount greater than 20 per cent of the contract price may lead to a d+ebarment from participating in future public pre-contractual procedures+;
* In the event of false statements in the tender, in addition to +possible+ +*criminal liability*+, there may be administrative offence liability, punishable by a +fine of up to €44,000.00+ and an ancillary sanction of a +*ban on participation in public procurement procedures for a maximum period of 2 years*+);
* Unlike the consequence set out in third point, the prohibition set out in fourth point, arising from an administrative offence, cannot be overcome.

Please let me know if you want to discuss.

