We need to figure out how to store the (drop-off option, instructions for courier) pairs in Elasticsearch. The dynamic template definition will likely need to be updated. Using the same one for nested object would result in an error as per the documentation [https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C05NDRKUYQ3/p1719327485190679?thread_ts=1717079175.946959&cid=C05NDRKUYQ3|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C05NDRKUYQ3/p1719327485190679?thread_ts=1717079175.946959&cid=C05NDRKUYQ3|smart-link] 

{quote}Note that the {{path_match}} and {{path_unmatch}} parameters match on object paths in addition to leaf fields. As an example, indexing the following document will result in an error because the {{path_match}} setting also matches the object field {{name.title}}, which can’t be mapped as text:{quote}

{noformat}PUT my-index-000001/_doc/2
{
  "name": {
    "first":  "Paul",
    "last":   "McCartney",
    "title": {
      "value": "Sir",
      "category": "order of chivalry"
    }
  }
}{noformat}



My initial thought is to use a combination of {{path_match}} and {{path_unmatch}}, something like:

{code:json}{
  "dynamic_templates": [
      {
          "final_delivery_address_form_template": {
              "path_match": "final_delivery_address_form.attributes.*",
              "path_unmatch": "final_delivery_address_form.attributes.dropoff_options",
              "path_match": "final_delivery_address_form.attributes.dropoff_options.*",
              "mapping": {
                  "type": "keyword"
              }
          }
      }
  ]
}{code}

(not tested ☝️)

But of course, the drawback here is that we would have to list every such nested object explicitly which kinda defeats the purpose of having the templates in the first place 😞



A different way to go about it is here [https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/32595#issuecomment-634233155|https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/32595#issuecomment-634233155|smart-link]. This seems better and looks like is exactly our use case.

